---
import InteriorLayout from '../../layouts/InteriorLayout.astro';
import StarDivider from '../../components/StarDivider.astro';
import PageWayfinding from '../../components/PageWayfinding.astro';
import SubstackPost from '../../components/SubstackPost.astro';
---
<InteriorLayout title='The Human Question | Raya Maia' description='The Human Question is a home for essays, briefs, and field notes on ideas worth sitting with, acting on, or simply noticing.' pageClass='page-writing' canonicalPath='/work/the-human-question/'>
<main>
<div class="page-shell">
<PageWayfinding position="top" />
<section class="sky-hero">
<div class="sky-hero-copy reveal">
<p class="kicker">
              Writing
            </p>
<h1>
              For questions that keep turning up where I least expect them.
            </h1>
<p class="lede">
              The Human Question is a home for ideas, observations, and the occasional rabbit hole.
			  <br/><br/>Here, the subject is rarely the whole story.
            </p>
</div>
</section>
<StarDivider />
<section class="field-section">
<div class="field-layout">
<aside class="field-index reveal">
<strong>01</strong>Featured
				</aside>
<div class="field-content reveal">
<h2>
					Where it began.
				  </h2>
<p>
					The first essay from <em>The Human Question</em> begins with a problem I keep returning to: what happens to our judgment 
					when we become very good at borrowing someone else’s?
				  </p>
</div>
</div>
<SubstackPost postUrl="https://rayamaia.substack.com/p/when-certainty-comes-secondhand" fallbackTitle="When Certainty Comes Secondhand" fallbackDeck="On trust, judgment, and what remains ours." />
</section>
<section class="field-section">
<div class="field-layout">
<aside class="field-index reveal">
<strong>02</strong>Three Forms
            </aside>
<div class="field-content reveal">
<h2>
                Three ways of following a question.
              </h2>
<p>
                What catches my attention and what I end up writing about are almost never the same. Sometimes a question deserves to be followed all the way down; 
				sometimes an idea asks to be put to work; sometimes an observation is worth capturing before it disappears. <em>The Human Question</em> 
				gives me room for all three.
              </p>
<article class="essay-object">
<p class="essay-meta">
                  Essays
                </p>
<h3>
                  The big Human Questions
                </h3>
<p>
                  A question worth sitting with. Longer pieces that follow an idea wherever it leads — through technology, education, 
				  culture, or the wonderfully complicated business of being human.
                </p>
</article>
<article class="essay-object">
<p class="essay-meta">
                  Briefs
                </p>
<h3>
                  Ideas that should affect what we do.
                </h3>
<p>
                  An idea worth acting on. Practical pieces that take an argument, problem, or emerging insight and ask what it 
				  should change in the real world.
                </p>
</article>
<article class="essay-object">
<p class="essay-meta">
                  Field Notes
                </p>
<h3>
                  Humans in the wild.
                </h3>
<p>
                  Something worth noticing. Shorter observations from the places where ideas actually meet people — classrooms, 
				  communities, technology, games, conversations, and everywhere in between.
                </p>
</article>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="field-section">
<div class="field-layout">
<aside class="field-index reveal">
<strong>03</strong>On writing
				</aside>
<div class="field-content reveal">
<h2>
					Why I write.
				  </h2>
<p>
					I wanted to blog long before I believed I had anything worth blogging about. For years, I assumed writing publicly 
					required grand ideas, unusual accomplishments, or at least some convincing reason that a stranger should care what I thought.
					<br/><br/>Eventually, I realized I had it backwards. Some of what intrigues me most lives in the 
					nothing-specials: a conversation, an observation, a question I can’t quite shake. The smallest details are often where the bigger ones become visible.
					<br/><br/>So, primarily, I write for myself. I want a record of how I saw the world at 23, and someday at 30, 40, or 50. 
					I want to be able to look back and see not only how technology and society changed around me, but how my own thinking changed with them.
					<br/><br/>I love when something I write resonates with someone else, starts a conversation, or gives another person a new way of looking 
					at something familiar. That makes writing more rewarding, but it isn’t what decides whether an idea belongs here.
					<br/><br/>I write about what I want to remember, what I want to understand, and what I’m not quite finished thinking about.
					</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<PageWayfinding position="bottom" />
</div>
</main>
</InteriorLayout>
